--TEST--
Core\SignedCookie::open takes a tainted cookie and answers a plain string, while Core\Jwt::verify answers tainted claims for a token signed under the same key one line earlier — the two halves of the one rule that a verified signature does not launder — `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`, `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The agreement shape, over a rule whose entire content is that two members of
// the same closed roster answer differently. `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder` says a verified
// signature does not launder — a JWT's claims come back `tainted` because a
// signature proves origin and not safety — and then names exactly one
// exception: a payload the application itself sealed comes back unqualified,
// because it was already plain when it went in. That reads like an oversight
// from either side alone, which is why it is asserted from both here.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/signed_cookie.rs`'s `open` launders section is the home
// of the reasoning.
//
// Both halves are asserted by the *declarations* below rather than by anything
// printed: neither one can be spelled the other way round and still compile, so
// this file compiling at all is the assertion and the output is the evidence
// that the values are the ones being talked about.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

// A cookie is read back out of a request header, so its text is untrusted
// before anything has looked at it. `open`'s `$cookie` is a laundering
// parameter, so a tainted value is what it is written to receive.
string $sealed = Core\SignedCookie::seal("id=42; role=admin", $ring);
tainted string $fromHeader = $sealed;

// And a plain `string` comes back out. A contagious row would refuse this
// declaration, and so would a row that answered `tainted string` the way the
// class beside it does.
string $payload = Core\SignedCookie::open($fromHeader, $ring);
echo $payload, "\n";

// The other half, under a key of the same kind and over the same text: a token
// this program signed a moment ago, verified by the member next to it on ADR
// 0060 § 1's roster, hands its claims back qualified. The signature is no
// weaker here — it is the same HMAC over the same key — so what the qualifier
// tracks is where the plaintext came from, which for a claim is an issuer and
// for a cookie payload was this program.
string $token = Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada", "role" => "admin"], 15m, $key);
array<tainted string> $claims = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $key);
tainted string $role = $claims["role"];

// So the rule is legible as code rather than as a comment: the pattern sink
// refuses a tainted argument outright, and which of these two values needs
// `Core\Regex::quote` in front of it is the whole of `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`. The cookie
// payload goes straight in.
Core\Regex\Pattern $direct = Core\Regex::compile($payload);
echo Core\Regex::matches("id=42; role=admin", $direct) ? "y" : "n", "\n";

// The claim cannot, even though it is a substring of the value the same file
// sealed and opened above — laundering it is a separate, written act.
string $quoted = Core\Regex::quote($role);
Core\Regex\Pattern $laundered = Core\Regex::compile($quoted);
echo Core\Regex::matches("admin", $laundered) ? "y" : "n", "\n";
echo $quoted, "\n";
--EXPECT--
id=42; role=admin
y
y
admin
